A||||8||JetroGT||Cleaning the gunk out of a plenum?||||5||
Z||000000||JetroGT||10-20-2001||08:43 AM||||I'm getting another 87GT with a 3.4 soon. I have an extra intake and valve covers in my closet that I want to clean and paint for the new engine.<BR>What's the best/easiest thing to use to get that black oily stuff out? Simple green just isn't strong enough.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Grime.jpg"> <P>--Suns coming up. I have to sleep so I don't catch on fire.--   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I hate working nights.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NewSigt.jpg">    <BR>I work where all the missing paperclips go.<BR>Wanted: 86/87 Red GT, auto, pw, pl, pm, rear defrost,pt, Gray int, Spoiler. May be willing to trade the blue car.<P>[This message has been edited by JetroGT (edited 10-20-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by JetroGT (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||grinthock||10-20-2001||10:43 AM||||Just spray the hell out of it with carb clean that stuff will dissolve, spray it, let it sit, then dump it out, keep doing it till the cleaner comes out clear.<P>Last time I cleaned mine I used a whole can of the stuff||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||10-20-2001||12:13 PM||||If you really want it clean just use aircraft paint stripper.  You can find it in pep boys.  Just dab it on, wait a while and rinse.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JetroGT||10-20-2001||04:10 PM||||Thanks! I never thought of using paint stripper as a degreaser. What ever works. I can probably use that to get the rest of that paint off. I ended up stripping the valve covers 3 times before all of the paint came off!||||reg||14
Z||000004||Cooter||10-20-2001||05:29 PM||||I got one of the big "tupperware" like boxes to use when stripping my intake. I put the intake in upside down and poured the stripper into the ports until the whole intake was covered by the stuff. It worked great, just took a lot of stripper though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||10-20-2001||09:33 PM||||or just take it to a sandblaster and have it bead blasted, leaves no residue and completely clean. cost me $10.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||10-20-2001||11:04 PM||||if you're talking about what's inside the runners would it br worth it to try extrude honing? especially since they are going on a 3.4. i'm not asking that with any sort of experience to back it up. it just occured to me that it might kill two birds with one stone.<P>or kill one bird and cook your goose.<P>or it would run like a scalded roadrunner.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||watts||10-21-2001||04:39 AM||||You can also TRY using the 'no bake' oven cleaner.  I've never used it on aluminum parts though, so do so at your own risk!<P>Better yet, try it on a small bit of scrap aluminum first.  <P>Works great as a degreaser on engines, metal firewalls, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||JetroGT||10-21-2001||07:34 AM||||I try soaking it in the paint stripper. I'll use a large disposable tin cooking pan. I want to do the covers in red and black, since I'm trying to make the car look like the model I made. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/minimodel.jpg"> <BR>It was a great way to test out my idea to see if I liked it or not.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
